te or so a flare goes up 110-250 m ahead (a streak of\nsparks, a soft pop), bursts 100-150 m up and sinks under its parachute for\nabout twenty-five seconds, drifting with the wind (flares.js).\n\nIts light on the ground is a directional light along its direction (far\nenough for parallel rays over the 80 m round the player), as bright as the\nflare is there and flickering: magnesium white over the warm lamps,\nglittering on every wet cobble. It casts shadows there - houses, walls,\nsoldiers, and the tree trunks, which cast only while a flare burns - that\nswing as it drifts and lengthen as it sinks. The 2048-square map is drawn\nonly while one burns, every other frame (about 0.8 ms a frame on an M1\nMax); the lamps' cube shadow holds still meanwhile. The depth shaders it\nneeds are compiled at the start of the mission, not at the first burst\n(which hitched 93 ms; now 14).\n\nIn the light pool it is a far light that lights no surface itself\n(lights.js: far, noLight), so it only puts a little glow in the wet air\n(volumetric.js now takes a per-light fog factor), lights the smoke and the\nrain; the cloud deck round it lights up (sky.js) and it trails smoke.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\ngit log --oneline -3","description":"Commit the illumination flares"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
